// automations.memql
//
// Epic C's two triggers -- one on a session subrun reaching `succeeded`, one
// schedule over the corpus -- and epic D's certification ladder (epic
// memql#5408): the template a trusted or canary procedure serves a goal
// through, the promotion decision, and the ladder's two sweeps.
//
// Nothing here ACTIVATES a learned procedure. A learned procedure is never an
// ordinary automation: it is served only through replayLearnedProcedure, only
// on a rung the ladder decides serves, and it reaches that rung only through
// shadow evidence and one person's approval.
//
// FOUR OF THE SIX RUN UNDER THE MAINTENANCE PRINCIPAL. Each one must read a
// row -- an approval, the list of owners -- BEFORE it can know whose it is,
// and every one of those rows declares a composite owner tier, so the default
// RoleReader system actor would read zero rows and no error: a ladder that
// never moves looks exactly like one with nothing to move. Each handler then
// does every other read under the owner's own actor. The entries, with their
// reasons, are in component/auth/maintenance_actor.go, compiled in so a
// mounted product bundle cannot grant itself one. The other two need none:
// learnFromSucceededRun's event carries the run's owner, which the handler
// borrows (and re-verifies with an owner-filtered read), and
// replayLearnedProcedure runs inside the goal's own run, as its owner.

/// On a run TRANSITIONING to `succeeded`, mine the corpus it belongs to -- and, once the lift is
/// done, compare the recording against every learned procedure in shadow for the same goal, which
/// is the evidence the ladder climbs on. The builtin itself decides whether there is anything to
/// learn: a corpus of fewer than two recordings answers `accepted: false` with the reason rather
/// than failing.
///
/// THE OWNER TRAVELS WITH THE EVENT. The handler borrows exactly that one owner's authority -- only
/// for this trusted completion trigger -- and re-reads the run under it, so an owner hint that does
/// not own the run reads nothing. A run with no owner or no goal signature has no corpus, and the
/// filter drops it before any handler runs.
///
/// THE FILTER NAMES THE TRANSITION, not merely the state. node.updated fires on every write to a
/// run, and a run keeps being written after it succeeds -- the retention sweep folds its summary
/// onto it -- so `row.status == "succeeded"` alone would fire again on each of those writes, and a
/// shadow comparison run twice for one recording would count one match as two. oldStatus is the
/// prior version's status, which the engine publishes on every update for exactly this; a prior
/// version with no status reads as unset, and unset is not `succeeded`, so a first write still
/// fires.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:work:run")
@filter(row => row.status == "succeeded" && args.oldStatus != "succeeded" && row.ownerUserId != "" && row.goalSignature != "")
automation learnFromSucceededRun {
  args {
    id           any
    oldStatus    any
    ownerUserId  any
  }

  learned := builtin procedureLearnFromRun(runId: args.id ?? "", ownerUserId: args.ownerUserId ?? "")
}

/// Every six hours: mine the second corpus level, where an automation invocation is a symbol like
/// any action, so a repeated sequence of automations becomes a higher automation by the same
/// compression rule (design D24). A cron firing carries no arguments, so ownerUserId arrives blank:
/// the automation runs under the maintenance principal, and the handler answers a blank owner from
/// THAT principal by iterating owners and mining each one's corpus under that owner's own actor. A
/// blank owner from anybody else is still refused, because a read with no actor returns zero rows
/// and no error -- which would look exactly like a cluster that has learned everything there is.
@trigger(schedule="0 0 */6 * * *")
automation mineProcedureCorpusAcrossAutomations {
  args {
    ownerUserId  any
  }

  mined := builtin procedureMineCorpus(ownerUserId: args.ownerUserId ?? "", level: 2)
}

/// Serve a goal from a learned procedure (epic memql#5408): the ONE template compile names when the
/// certification ladder says a procedure serves -- trusted, or canary with the app standing by.
///
/// @template, NOT @trigger: a run that NAMED it invokes it, the way invokeAgent and produceArtifact
/// are reached, and the load-time gate refuses a template that also carries a trigger. Compile
/// writes no templateConstructId for this route, so the run executes this embedded automation, and
/// passes the construct as procedureConstructId in the run's variables. Everything else is
/// procedureReplay's, including the one check compile cannot make: the rung is re-read at
/// execution, so a procedure demoted or re-lifted since compile chose it falls back to the app.
@template
automation replayLearnedProcedure {
  args {
    /// The learned procedure compile chose: a construct owned by the run's owner, on canary or
    /// trusted when compile read it.
    procedureConstructId  string!
  }

  replayed := builtin procedureReplay(constructId: args.procedureConstructId)
}

/// When a procedurePromotion approval is DECIDED, apply the decision to the ladder: approved moves
/// the procedure from shadow to canary, rejected keeps it in shadow to re-earn the proposal. The
/// filter reads the kind and a non-empty decision, so neither the approval being raised nor any
/// other kind of gate fires it; the handler is idempotent, so a second delivery of the same
/// decision changes nothing.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:work:approval")
@filter(row => row.kind == "procedurePromotion" && row.decision != "")
automation onProcedurePromotionDecided {
  args {
    id  any
  }

  decided := builtin procedureDecidePromotion(approvalId: args.id ?? "")
}

/// Every fifteen minutes: re-evaluate every canary and trusted procedure's stored evidence against
/// the CURRENT ladder policy. A replay already applies the policy to the procedure it served; this
/// is what reaches a procedure that is not being served -- a threshold that tightened, or evidence
/// written before it did -- so a procedure that no longer clears the policy stops serving within a
/// quarter of an hour rather than at its next failure.
@trigger(schedule="0 */15 * * * *")
automation demoteProcedures {
  sweep := builtin procedureLadderSweep(sweep: "demotion")
}

/// Nightly 03:30 UTC: retire every learned procedure unused for longer than the policy's window, on
/// any rung. Retirement sets the rung and leaves the construct's lifecycle status alone, so a
/// retired procedure is still shown and still readable -- it simply stops being served.
@trigger(schedule="0 30 3 * * *")
automation retireProcedures {
  sweep := builtin procedureLadderSweep(sweep: "retirement")
}
